E.append(cortex("gyrus-inferior-temporal-temporooccipital", "Inferior temporal gyrus, temporo-occipital part", "temporal",
 "The temporo-occipital part of the inferior temporal gyrus is the most posterior segment of the lower lateral temporal cortex, where it passes into the inferior lateral occipital cortex behind the level of the atrium. It contains shape-selective and object-selective visual cortex (the lateral occipital complex), contributes to the visual word form system in the dominant hemisphere and to face and body perception on the right, and is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery with a middle cerebral artery contribution at its upper edge. Lesions produce apperceptive visual agnosia, alexia and, when the adjacent optic radiation is involved, a contralateral upper quadrant defect.",
 "Posterior end of the inferior temporal gyrus at the junction with the occipital lobe, lateral to the occipital fusiform gyrus and below the temporo-occipital part of the middle temporal gyrus, above the inferior surface of the hemisphere.",
 "This cortex assembles edges, contours and surfaces into whole object shapes and represents them independently of low-level features, the stage at which a drawn outline and a photograph become the same object; the left side contributes to recognizing letter strings as words and the right to recognizing faces and bodies as wholes. It receives the ventral occipital areas and projects forward along the inferior longitudinal fasciculus to the inferior temporal and perirhinal cortex and to the amygdala. Damage produces apperceptive agnosia, in which the patient cannot copy or match shapes, in contrast to the associative agnosia of more anterior lesions where copying is preserved.",
 PCA + MCA_I,
 [mview("axial", "gyrus-inferior-temporal-temporooccipital-l", "inferior temporo-occipital junction behind the atrium"), mview("coronal", "gyrus-inferior-temporal-temporooccipital-r", "lateral occipito-temporal cortex above the inferior surface")],
 "Lateral cortex at the inferior temporo-occipital junction on coronal images behind the atrium, lateral to the occipitotemporal sulcus; the preoccipital notch marks its lower posterior border.",
 [pathol("PCA branch infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the inferior temporo-occipital cortex with visual agnosia or pure alexia (left) and a superior quadrantanopia.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Posterior cortical atrophy", "MRI", "Occipito-temporal atrophy with impaired object and face recognition and reading; FDG-PET shows posterior hypometabolism.", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("Occipito-temporal cortical dysplasia", "MRI", "Focal cortical thickening at the temporo-occipital junction in a patient with visual auras of shapes and colors.", sequence="FLAIR")],
 [("Apperceptive visual agnosia", "n/a", "Loss of shape integration in the lateral occipital complex"), ("Alexia and impaired letter recognition (left)", "n/a", "Damage to the visual word form pathway from the occipital lobe to the fusiform gyrus"), ("Contralateral superior quadrantanopia", "contralateral", "Involvement of Meyer's loop fibers of the optic radiation in the underlying white matter")],
 ["Copying and matching of shapes and letters (fails in apperceptive agnosia)", "Reading single words and letters; writing is preserved in pure alexia", "Quadrantic visual fields"],
 ["Copying tells apperceptive from associative agnosia: the apperceptive patient cannot copy, the associative one copies but cannot recognize", "Alexia without agraphia arises when the left occipito-temporal cortex and the splenial fibers are cut together", "Upper quadrant defects come from the temporal loop of the optic radiation, which runs under this cortex"],
 [R("sp-agnosia"), R("sp-visual-cortex"), R("sp-occipital-lobe")], synonyms=["lateral occipital complex", "posterior ITG"], territories=["territory-pca-temporal", "territory-pca-occipital"], pathways=["pathway-visual"], syndromes=["syn-alexia-without-agraphia", "syn-pca-infarct"],
 afferents=[("Ventral occipital cortex (V2-V4)", "short association fibers"), ("Primary visual cortex", "occipito-temporal projection"), ("Contralateral occipito-temporal cortex", "splenium")],
 efferents=[("Posterior and anterior inferior temporal cortex", "inferior longitudinal fasciculus"), ("Angular gyrus", "short association fibers"), ("Amygdala", "temporo-amygdalar fibers")],
 pitfalls=["A patient who cannot read but writes normally has a disconnection, not a language disorder; check the splenium and left occipital lobe"], tags=["ventral stream", "shape", "reading"]))

E.append(cortex("planum-polare", "Planum polare", "temporal",
 "The planum polare is the smooth superior surface of the anterior temporal lobe in front of Heschl's gyrus, forming the floor of the anterior part of the lateral sulcus beneath the frontal operculum and facing the anterior insula. It is auditory association cortex of the anterior belt and parabelt (part of area 22) and it processes the temporal structure of sounds, melody and the prosody of voices, feeding the anterior stream of sound identification. It is a middle cerebral artery inferior division territory, is involved with the insula in operculo-insular seizures, and is part of the auditory cortex that is spared in cortical deafness only when both Heschl's gyri survive.",
 "Anterior part of the upper surface of the superior temporal gyrus, hidden within the lateral sulcus between the temporal pole in front, Heschl's gyrus behind, the insula medially and the frontal operculum above.",
 "The planum polare contains the anterior auditory belt fields that respond to the pitch contour and rhythm of sounds, to melodies and to the emotional tone of the voice, particularly on the right, and it feeds the anterior stream of sound identification that runs to the temporal pole and the inferior frontal gyrus. It also receives visceral and gustatory information from the adjacent insula and participates in the integration of hearing with emotion through its amygdala connections. Seizures involving it produce auditory auras of distorted sound or music, and lesions produce amusia and impaired recognition of the emotional intonation of speech.",
 MCA_I,
 [mview("coronal", "planum-polare-l", "planum polare in the floor of the anterior Sylvian fissure"), mview("sagittal", "planum-polare-r", "upper surface of the anterior temporal lobe facing the insula")],
 "The flat upper surface of the anterior temporal lobe seen on coronal images through the anterior insula, beneath the Sylvian cistern; it slopes upward toward Heschl's gyrus posteriorly.",
 [pathol("MCA inferior division infarct", "MRI", "Cortical restricted diffusion on the superior temporal surface and the adjacent insula with impaired recognition of prosody and music.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Operculo-insular seizures", "EEG", "Auditory distortion, music or voices at onset with fear or a throat sensation; scalp EEG is often equivocal.", sequence="n/a"),
  pathol("Sylvian arachnoid cyst", "MRI", "CSF-signal cyst in the anterior Sylvian fissure flattening the planum polare and displacing the temporal pole, usually incidental.", sequence="T2")],
 [("Amusia and impaired recognition of emotional prosody (right)", "n/a", "Loss of the anterior belt cortex processing pitch contour and melody"), ("Impaired identification of voices and complex sounds", "n/a", "Disruption of the anterior auditory identification stream"), ("Auditory illusions and hallucinations during seizures", "n/a", "Ictal activation of anterior auditory association cortex")],
 ["Ask the patient to hum a familiar tune and to recognize melodies", "Identify the emotion in sentences spoken with varied intonation", "Seizure history of distorted sounds or music"],
 ["Music and the melody of speech are right anterior temporal; words are left posterior temporal", "A Sylvian arachnoid cyst compressing the planum polare is usually silent and needs no surgery", "Operculo-insular seizures with auditory and throat symptoms are among the most misdiagnosed focal seizures"],
 [R("sp-auditory-pathway"), R("sp-sylvian-fissure")], synonyms=["anterior superior temporal plane"], territories=["territory-mca-temporal", "territory-mca-insular"], pathways=["pathway-auditory"], syndromes=["syn-mca-inferior-division", "syn-hearing-loss"],
 afferents=[("Heschl's gyrus (core auditory cortex)", "short association fibers"), ("Medial geniculate nucleus (belt projection)", "acoustic radiation"), ("Anterior insula", "short association fibers")],
 efferents=[("Temporal pole and anterior superior temporal gyrus", "short association fibers"), ("Inferior frontal gyrus", "uncinate and extreme capsule fibers"), ("Amygdala", "temporo-amygdalar fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Aprosodia can be mistaken for depression or flat affect; test recognition of intonation separately from mood"], tags=["auditory belt", "prosody", "music"]))

E.append(cortex("planum-temporale", "Planum temporale", "temporal",
 "The planum temporale is the flat triangular surface of the superior temporal gyrus behind Heschl's gyrus, forming the floor of the posterior lateral sulcus beneath the parietal operculum. It is larger on the left in most people, one of the earliest described anatomical asymmetries of the language brain, and it contains auditory association cortex that decodes the spectral and temporal structure of speech and locates sounds in space, forming the start of the dorsal auditory stream to Wernicke's area, the supramarginal gyrus and the frontal speech cortex. It is supplied by the inferior division of the middle cerebral artery, and lesions contribute to Wernicke and conduction aphasia, to impaired sound localization, and to the auditory hallucinations of schizophrenia, in which its asymmetry is reduced.",
 "Posterior part of the superior surface of the temporal lobe, behind the transverse temporal gyrus of Heschl and in front of the point where the lateral sulcus turns upward into the supramarginal gyrus; it faces the parietal operculum across the posterior Sylvian fissure.",
 "The planum temporale analyzes the rapidly changing spectral patterns of speech and other complex sounds and computes where a sound comes from and whether it is moving; in the left hemisphere it is the entry point of the dorsal stream that maps phonology onto articulation through the arcuate fasciculus, and it contains a sensorimotor interface used in repetition and in monitoring one's own voice. It is also engaged when reading silently, when listening to music, and in the perception of the timing of sounds. Its size asymmetry relates to language lateralization but is only loosely tied to handedness.",
 MCA_I,
 [mview("axial", "planum-temporale-l", "planum temporale behind Heschl's gyrus on the superior temporal surface"), mview("coronal", "planum-temporale-r", "floor of the posterior Sylvian fissure beneath the parietal operculum")],
 "Flat superior temporal surface behind the transverse temporal gyrus on axial images through the level of the thalamus and posterior insula; the left planum is longer and reaches further back in most brains.",
 [pathol("Conduction or Wernicke aphasia from a posterior MCA branch infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion in the posterior superior temporal plane and the underlying arcuate fibers with impaired repetition and phonemic paraphasias.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Cortical deafness or auditory agnosia", "MRI", "Bilateral posterior superior temporal lesions, usually sequential strokes, with inability to interpret sounds despite normal audiometry.", sequence="FLAIR"),
  pathol("Reduced planum asymmetry (research finding)", "MRI", "Loss of the normal left-greater-than-right planum size in schizophrenia and dyslexia; not diagnostic in an individual.", sequence="T1")],
 [("Impaired repetition with phonemic paraphasias", "n/a", "Damage to the sensorimotor speech interface at the start of the dorsal stream"), ("Impaired sound localization in the contralateral hemispace", "contralateral", "Loss of the spatial auditory processing of the posterior temporal plane"), ("Contribution to word deafness when bilateral", "bilateral", "Loss of speech-sound decoding in both auditory association cortices")],
 ["Repetition of words and non-words and of long sentences", "Localization of sounds made behind the patient on each side", "Comprehension of speech in noise, which fails early with posterior temporal lesions"],
 ["Repetition failure with fluent speech and good comprehension points to the posterior superior temporal plane and the arcuate fasciculus", "The left planum temporale is the classic anatomical signature of language dominance, visible on a single MRI", "Cortical deafness needs both plana and Heschl's gyri; a unilateral lesion never makes a patient deaf"],
 [R("sp-wernicke-area"), R("sp-auditory-pathway"), R("sp-sylvian-fissure")], synonyms=["posterior superior temporal plane", "area 22/42"], territories=["territory-mca-temporal"], pathways=["pathway-auditory"], syndromes=["syn-aphasia-conduction-global", "syn-aphasia-wernicke", "syn-hearing-loss"],
 afferents=[("Heschl's gyrus (core auditory cortex)", "short association fibers"), ("Medial geniculate nucleus", "acoustic radiation (belt input)"), ("Contralateral planum temporale", "corpus callosum")],
 efferents=[("Supramarginal gyrus and inferior frontal gyrus", "arcuate fasciculus"), ("Posterior superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke area)", "short association fibers"), ("Posterior parietal cortex (auditory space)", "short association fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Conduction aphasia is missed unless repetition is tested with sentences of several words"], tags=["auditory association", "dorsal stream", "asymmetry"]))

E.append(cortex("cortex-temporal-fusiform-anterior", "Temporal fusiform cortex, anterior division", "temporal",
 "The anterior fusiform cortex is the front part of the fusiform (lateral occipitotemporal) gyrus on the inferior surface of the temporal lobe, between the collateral sulcus medially and the occipitotemporal sulcus laterally, beneath the hippocampus and lateral to the parahippocampal gyrus. It is high-order ventral visual cortex that hands object and face identity to the perirhinal cortex and the anterior temporal semantic system, and it is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery through its temporal branches. It atrophies with the temporal pole in semantic dementia, it is the fusiform cortex most often removed in temporal lobectomy, and its lesions contribute to prosopagnosia, associative agnosia and anomia.",
 "Anterior portion of the fusiform gyrus on the basal temporal surface, medial to the inferior temporal gyrus, lateral to the parahippocampal gyrus and the collateral sulcus, under the anterior hippocampus and amygdala.",
 "Anterior fusiform neurons respond to specific familiar objects, faces and written words in a form independent of the image, and their output to the perirhinal and entorhinal cortex allows a seen object to be matched with its memory and its meaning; in the dominant hemisphere it is part of the basal temporal language area that supports naming and reading, and in the right it links a face to the person's identity. It receives the posterior fusiform and inferior temporal cortex through the inferior longitudinal fasciculus and the amygdala, and it projects to the temporal pole and the orbitofrontal cortex. Electrical stimulation here disrupts naming and reading.",
 PCA,
 [mview("coronal", "cortex-temporal-fusiform-anterior-l", "anterior fusiform gyrus between the collateral and occipitotemporal sulci"), mview("axial", "cortex-temporal-fusiform-anterior-r", "basal temporal surface under the anterior hippocampus")],
 "The gyrus between the collateral sulcus and the occipitotemporal sulcus on the inferior temporal surface, seen on coronal images through the amygdala and the head of the hippocampus.",
 [pathol("PCA temporal branch infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the basal temporal cortex with associative agnosia, anomia or prosopagnosia and often a hemianopia.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Semantic dementia", "MRI", "Anterior fusiform and inferior temporal atrophy with the temporal pole, asymmetric and progressive, with loss of the meaning of objects and words.", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("Temporal lobe epilepsy (basal temporal onset)", "EEG", "Seizures with ictal anomia, reading arrest or complex visual auras; scalp EEG shows anterior temporal discharges.", sequence="n/a")],
 [("Associative visual agnosia and anomia for seen objects (left)", "n/a", "Disconnection of fusiform object representations from the semantic and lexical systems"), ("Prosopagnosia (right or bilateral)", "n/a", "Loss of the face-identity link between the fusiform cortex and the anterior temporal person nodes"), ("Loss of object knowledge when combined with polar atrophy", "bilateral", "Degeneration of the anterior temporal semantic hub")],
 ["Naming and identification of objects and famous faces", "Reading of words and letters", "Recognition by other modalities to prove the deficit is visual"],
 ["The basal temporal language area lies on the underside of the dominant temporal lobe; stimulation here stops naming during awake surgery", "The anterior fusiform gyrus bridges seeing and knowing: its loss leaves the patient describing an object without recognizing it", "Prosopagnosia from a unilateral lesion is right-sided; left fusiform lesions cause reading and naming problems instead"],
 [R("sp-prosopagnosia"), R("sp-agnosia"), R("sp-primary-progressive-aphasia")], synonyms=["anterior fusiform gyrus", "lateral occipitotemporal gyrus (anterior)"], territories=["territory-pca-temporal"], pathways=["pathway-visual"], syndromes=["syn-pca-infarct", "syn-kluver-bucy"],
 afferents=[("Posterior fusiform and inferior temporal cortex", "inferior longitudinal fasciculus"), ("Amygdala", "amygdalotemporal fibers"), ("Pulvinar", "thalamocortical fibers")],
 efferents=[("Perirhinal and entorhinal cortex", "short association fibers"), ("Temporal pole", "short association fibers"), ("Orbitofrontal cortex", "uncinate fasciculus")],
 pitfalls=["Basal temporal cortex is poorly seen on axial images because it is cut tangentially; coronal slices through the hippocampus show it properly"], tags=["fusiform", "ventral stream", "basal temporal language area"]))

E.append(cortex("cortex-temporal-fusiform-posterior", "Temporal fusiform cortex, posterior division", "temporal",
 "The posterior temporal fusiform cortex is the middle segment of the fusiform gyrus on the inferior temporal surface, beneath the body of the hippocampus and the atrium, between the collateral sulcus and the occipitotemporal sulcus. It contains the fusiform face area on the right and the visual word form area on the left, the two best-known category-selective regions of the ventral visual stream, and it is supplied by temporal branches of the posterior cerebral artery. Right-sided lesions cause prosopagnosia, left-sided lesions cause pure alexia when the splenial input is also cut, and bilateral disease produces the visual agnosias of posterior cortical atrophy.",
 "Middle third of the fusiform gyrus, medial to the inferior temporal gyrus and lateral to the parahippocampal gyrus, under the body and tail of the hippocampus at the level of the atrium, in front of the occipital fusiform gyrus.",
 "The right posterior fusiform cortex responds preferentially to faces, extracting the configuration of features that identifies an individual, while the left posterior fusiform cortex learns to recognize letter strings and familiar words as visual wholes, allowing fast reading; neighboring patches prefer bodies, places and tools. Both sides receive the occipital form areas and project forward to the anterior fusiform and perirhinal cortex and to the amygdala, and both are modulated by attention from the parietal and frontal cortex. Seizures arising here produce visual auras of faces or letters, and functional imaging of these areas is used to map eloquent cortex before temporo-occipital surgery.",
 PCA,
 [mview("coronal", "cortex-temporal-fusiform-posterior-l", "posterior fusiform gyrus under the body of the hippocampus"), mview("axial", "cortex-temporal-fusiform-posterior-r", "fusiform face region on the basal temporal surface")],
 "The fusiform gyrus on coronal images through the atrium, between the collateral and occipitotemporal sulci; the fusiform face area sits at its posterior end on the right and the visual word form area in the corresponding position on the left.",
 [pathol("Right PCA branch infarct with prosopagnosia", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the right posterior fusiform gyrus, often with a left superior quadrantanopia from the adjacent radiation.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Pure alexia", "MRI", "Left occipito-temporal infarct involving the posterior fusiform cortex and the splenial fibers; the patient reads letter by letter but writes normally.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Posterior cortical atrophy", "MRI", "Bilateral fusiform and occipito-parietal atrophy with prosopagnosia, alexia and simultanagnosia; hippocampi preserved.", sequence="T1")],
 [("Prosopagnosia (right)", "n/a", "Loss of the fusiform face area configuration processing"), ("Pure alexia and letter-by-letter reading (left)", "n/a", "Loss of the visual word form area or its input"), ("Contralateral superior quadrantanopia", "contralateral", "Optic radiation fibers in the adjacent white matter")],
 ["Face recognition of family and famous people; matching faces with different expressions", "Reading words of increasing length; letter-by-letter reading is the signature of pure alexia", "Quadrantic visual fields"],
 ["Reading time that grows with word length is the fingerprint of the visual word form area lesion", "The face area is right-sided: a right occipito-temporal stroke makes the patient recognize people by voice and gait", "Both regions are late developing and learnable: reading recruits a cortex that evolution built for objects"],
 [R("sp-temporal-lobe"), R("sp-prosopagnosia"), R("sp-visual-cortex")], synonyms=["fusiform face area (right)", "visual word form area (left)", "middle fusiform gyrus"], territories=["territory-pca-temporal", "territory-pca-occipital"], pathways=["pathway-visual"], syndromes=["syn-alexia-without-agraphia", "syn-pca-infarct"],
 afferents=[("Occipital fusiform and lateral occipital cortex (V4, LOC)", "short association fibers"), ("Contralateral fusiform cortex", "splenium of the corpus callosum"), ("Parietal and frontal attention areas", "top-down modulation via the inferior fronto-occipital fasciculus")],
 efferents=[("Anterior fusiform and perirhinal cortex", "inferior longitudinal fasciculus"), ("Amygdala", "temporo-amygdalar fibers"), ("Angular gyrus and posterior temporal language cortex (left)", "short association fibers")],
 pitfalls=["Prosopagnosic patients often deny a problem because they recognize people by other cues; ask whether they recognize themselves in photographs"], tags=["fusiform face area", "visual word form area", "ventral stream"]))

E.append(cortex("cortex-temporo-occipital-fusiform", "Temporal occipital fusiform cortex", "temporal",
 "The temporo-occipital fusiform cortex is the posterior segment of the fusiform gyrus where the temporal lobe meets the occipital lobe on the inferior surface, between the collateral sulcus and the occipitotemporal sulcus behind the level of the atrium. It is ventral extrastriate cortex in which color, texture and shape are combined (the human V4 complex and the posterior parts of the face and word areas) and it feeds the fusiform recognition regions in front of it. It lies squarely in the posterior cerebral artery territory beneath the calcarine cortex, so its infarction accompanies a hemianopia and adds achromatopsia, apperceptive agnosia or alexia; bilateral damage produces cerebral achromatopsia.",
 "Posterior end of the fusiform gyrus at the temporo-occipital junction, medial to the inferior lateral occipital cortex and lateral to the lingual gyrus and collateral sulcus, on the inferior surface of the hemisphere above the tentorium.",
 "This ventral occipito-temporal cortex computes the surface color and texture of objects independent of illumination, integrates contours into shapes and begins the category-specific organization that becomes face, word, body and place areas further forward; it is retinotopically organized with the contralateral upper field represented on the lower bank, and the right side dominates for global form and color. It receives the calcarine cortex and V2 through short fibers and projects along the inferior longitudinal fasciculus; the tentorium and the PCA branches lie directly beneath it. Lesions produce contralateral hemiachromatopsia and upper quadrant defects, and ictal activation gives colored visual auras.",
 PCA,
 [mview("coronal", "cortex-temporo-occipital-fusiform-l", "temporo-occipital fusiform gyrus above the tentorium"), mview("axial", "cortex-temporo-occipital-fusiform-r", "ventral occipito-temporal cortex lateral to the lingual gyrus")],
 "The fusiform gyrus behind the atrium on coronal images, below the lateral ventricle and above the tentorial surface, with the collateral sulcus separating it from the lingual gyrus medially.",
 [pathol("PCA infarct with achromatopsia", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the ventral occipito-temporal cortex below the calcarine sulcus with loss of color perception in the contralateral hemifield and an upper quadrantanopia.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Cerebral achromatopsia (bilateral)", "MRI", "Bilateral ventral occipito-temporal lesions, typically after sequential PCA strokes or hypotension, with the world seen in shades of gray.", sequence="FLAIR"),
  pathol("Occipital seizures with colored auras", "EEG", "Colored spots or shapes at onset with a posterior temporo-occipital focus; migraine aura is the main mimic.", sequence="n/a")],
 [("Contralateral hemiachromatopsia", "contralateral", "Loss of the color-constancy cortex of the ventral occipito-temporal region"), ("Apperceptive agnosia and impaired shape perception", "contralateral", "Loss of ventral extrastriate form processing"), ("Contralateral superior quadrantanopia", "contralateral", "Involvement of the lower calcarine bank or the underlying optic radiation")],
 ["Color naming and matching in each hemifield separately (pseudoisochromatic plates presented to each side)", "Copying and matching of shapes", "Visual fields by quadrant"],
 ["Loss of color in one hemifield with preserved acuity is the ventral occipito-temporal cortex; the patient may say the left side of the room looks washed out", "Colored auras are occipital, not migraine, when they last seconds and recur stereotypically", "The temporo-occipital fusiform cortex lies on the tentorium: tentorial meningiomas and PCA aneurysm compression present with these deficits"],
 [R("sp-prosopagnosia"), R("sp-visual-cortex"), R("sp-alexia")], synonyms=["posterior fusiform gyrus", "V4 complex"], territories=["territory-pca-occipital", "territory-pca-temporal"], pathways=["pathway-visual"], syndromes=["syn-pca-infarct", "syn-homonymous-hemianopia"],
 afferents=[("Primary and secondary visual cortex (V1, V2)", "short association fibers"), ("Pulvinar", "thalamocortical fibers"), ("Contralateral ventral occipital cortex", "splenium")],
 efferents=[("Posterior temporal fusiform cortex (face and word areas)", "inferior longitudinal fasciculus"), ("Inferior temporal gyrus", "short association fibers"), ("Posterior parietal cortex", "vertical occipital fasciculus")],
 pitfalls=["Hemiachromatopsia is never found unless colors are tested in each hemifield; standard color plates presented centrally miss it"], tags=["V4", "color", "ventral stream"]))

write(E)
